## Working Practices

Before asking the user to report state, check whether it is observable from the files, the config, or git history, and work it out directly.

Read the data, compute what each competing hypothesis predicts, and present the distinguishing outcome so a single glance resolves it. Git history counts as evidence — a diff of a file the app wrote reveals how the app mutates it.

Reserve questions for what genuinely exists only on their screen.

When working through a batch, do not interrupt mid-way with questions. Resolve every unambiguous case directly, accumulate the judgment calls, and ask them together in one round near the end.

Documents stay dry. Never lift data out of one document to aggregate it into another — reference the source instead.

This covers schemas, command invocations, algorithm walkthroughs, timestamps, results tables, and derived counts. A copied fact has two homes, and the copy goes stale silently. If something seems worth reproducing for convenience, that is exactly the copy that will rot.

When presenting hardware specs against a tool's requirements, state that the specs came from running a script on this machine rather than from assumption or lookup.
